::STAT_U03_L03_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
What is the formula for a residual?
{
~A. predicted value - actual value#This reverses the sign of the residual.
=B. actual value - predicted value#Residual measures the actual value minus the model prediction.
~C. actual value + predicted value#Adding does not measure prediction error.
~D. actual value x predicted value#Multiplication does not calculate residuals.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Actual value is 75 and predicted value is 70. What is the residual?
{
=A. 5#75 - 70 = 5.
~B. -5#This reverses the subtraction.
~C. 145#This adds the values.
~D. 70#This is the predicted value, not the residual.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Actual value is 43 and predicted value is 50. What is the residual?
{
=A. -7#43 - 50 = -7.
~B. 7#This reverses the subtraction; actual - predicted is negative.
~C. 93#This adds the values.
~D. 50#This is the predicted value.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
A positive residual means what?
{
~A. The actual value is below the prediction#That describes a negative residual.
~B. The model predicted exactly#Exact prediction gives residual 0.
~C. The model cannot be used#A positive residual alone does not make a model unusable.
=D. The actual value is above the prediction#Actual - predicted is positive when actual is larger.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
A negative residual means what?
{
~A. The actual value is above the prediction#That describes a positive residual.
~B. The residual must be ignored#Negative residuals are important for model fit.
~C. The prediction is always useless#A negative residual shows direction of error, not automatic model failure.
=D. The actual value is below the prediction#Actual - predicted is negative when actual is smaller.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which residual shows the closest prediction?
{
=A. 1#Residuals closest to 0 show the smallest prediction error.
~B. -12#This is farther from 0 than the correct answer.
~C. 8#This is farther from 0 than 1.
~D. 15#This is much farther from 0.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which residual shows the largest error?
{
~A. 2#This is a small error.
=B. -14#Error size is judged by distance from 0, and 14 is largest.
~C. -3#This is smaller in absolute value than 14.
~D. 0#A residual of 0 means no error for that point.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
What residual plot pattern supports a linear model?
{
~A. A U-shaped pattern#A U-shape suggests a line may not fit well.
~B. Residuals steadily increasing#A trend in residuals suggests missed structure.
=C. Random scatter around 0#No clear residual pattern supports a linear model.
~D. Residuals all far from 0#Large residuals suggest poor predictions.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
If residuals form a curve, what does that suggest?
{
~A. The line fits perfectly#A perfect fit would have residuals at 0.
~B. The residual formula is unnecessary#Residuals are what reveal the pattern.
~C. The variables are categorical#Residuals here are used with numerical model predictions.
=D. A linear model may not be appropriate#A curved residual pattern suggests the relationship may not be linear.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Actual value is 100 and predicted value is 100. What is the residual?
{
~A. 200#This adds the values.
~B. 1#There is no difference between actual and predicted.
=C. 0#100 - 100 = 0.
~D. -100#This does not follow actual - predicted.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q11::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
What does a residual of -4 mean in context?
{
~A. The actual value was 4 above the prediction#That would be residual +4.
=B. The actual value was 4 below the prediction#A negative residual means actual is below predicted.
~C. The prediction was exact#Exact prediction gives residual 0.
~D. The model has no error#A residual of -4 shows an error of 4 units.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q12::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Why are residuals useful?
{
~A. They prove causation#Residuals evaluate prediction error, not causation.
=B. They show how far predictions are from actual values#Residuals measure prediction error.
~C. They replace the original data#Residuals help analyze the model but do not replace the data.
~D. They make every model linear#Residuals help judge whether a linear model fits.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q13::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Actual value is 64 and predicted value is 59. What is the residual?
{
~A. -5#This reverses the subtraction.
~B. 123#This adds the values.
=C. 5#64 - 59 = 5.
~D. 59#This is the predicted value.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q14::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which statement about residuals is true?
{
~A. Residuals are always positive#Residuals can be positive, negative, or 0.
~B. Residuals are only for categorical data#Residuals are used with numerical model predictions.
~C. Residuals never help evaluate fit#Residuals are a main tool for evaluating model fit.
=D. Residuals can be positive, negative, or 0#The sign depends on actual - predicted.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q15::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which residual plot suggests poor linear fit?
{
~A. Points scattered randomly around 0#This supports a linear model.
~B. Many residuals close to 0#Small residuals suggest close predictions.
=C. A clear wave pattern#A pattern suggests the line is missing structure.
~D. A mix of positive and negative residuals with no pattern#This is expected for a reasonable linear fit.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q16::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
If a model underpredicts an actual value, what sign is the residual?
{
~A. Negative#Underprediction means actual is greater than predicted.
~B. Zero#Zero means exact prediction.
~C. Impossible to know if actual and predicted are known#The sign can be known from actual - predicted.
=D. Positive#If actual is greater than predicted, actual - predicted is positive.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q17::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
If a model overpredicts an actual value, what sign is the residual?
{
~A. Positive#Positive means actual is above prediction.
=B. Negative#Actual is less than predicted, so actual - predicted is negative.
~C. Zero#Zero means exact prediction.
~D. Always 1#Residual size depends on the values.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q18::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Actual value is 28 and predicted value is 35. What is the residual?
{
~A. 7#This reverses actual and predicted.
~B. 63#This adds the values.
=C. -7#28 - 35 = -7.
~D. 35#This is the predicted value.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q19::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
What does "model fit" mean?
{
~A. The color of the line on a graph#Color does not determine fit.
~B. The name of the data set#The name does not evaluate predictions.
=C. How well the model matches the data#Model fit describes how well predictions align with actual values.
~D. Whether the data are written in a table#Tables can hold data but do not define model fit.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q20::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which residual is closest to 0?
{
~A. -9#This is farther from 0 than 2.
=B. 2#Distance from 0 is smallest for 2.
~C. 6#This is farther from 0 than 2.
~D. -5#This is farther from 0 than 2.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q21::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which residual has the same error size as -6?
{
=A. 6#Both -6 and 6 are 6 units from 0.
~B. -3#This has error size 3.
~C. 0#This has no error.
~D. 12#This has error size 12.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q22::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Residuals all far above 0 would suggest what?
{
=A. The model predicted too low#Positive residuals mean the model underpredicted.
~B. The model predicted too high#If actual - predicted is positive, actual values are above predictions.
~C. The model is exact#Exact predictions have residual 0.
~D. The data are categorical#Residuals are numerical prediction errors.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q23::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Residuals all far below 0 would suggest what?
{
~A. The model predicted too low#Too low would create positive residuals.
~B. The model is exact#Exact predictions have residual 0.
~C. The model has no predictions#Residuals require predictions.
=D. The model predicted too high#Negative residuals mean actual values are below predictions.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q24::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Which step comes first when calculating a residual?
{
=A. Identify the actual and predicted values#You need both values before subtracting.
~B. Add actual and predicted#Residuals are differences, not sums.
~C. Square the predicted value#Squaring is not part of residual calculation here.
~D. Ignore the actual value#The actual value is needed.
}

::STAT_U03_L03_Q25::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L03_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
What is the best conclusion if most residuals are small and randomly scattered?
{
=A. The linear model appears to fit reasonably well#Small, patternless residuals support a linear model.
~B. The linear model is definitely causal#Residuals do not prove causation.
~C. The model must be replaced by a curved model#A curved model is suggested when residuals show a curve.
~D. The data have no variables#Residuals come from paired numerical variables and predictions.
}
